The test constructs `Dropout(rate=0.35)` with no `seed`, then computes `output_tensor` by calling `layer(inputs, training=True)`. In `Dropout.call`, when `training` is true and `rate > 0`, it applies `backend.random.dropout(...)`, and the layer docs describe this as randomly zeroing inputs during training. Because the test does not set a fixed seed and does not enforce equality of values across runs, the same input can produce different dropout masks and thus different `output_tensor` values on repeated executions.

